From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Thu, 04 Aug 1994 23:11:53 GMT
Subject: [#94-8-58] FAQ: Request For Comments, version 0.02

Eric Watt Forste asks:
> Is "extropy" the opposite of "entropy"?

I've not seen any answers yet, so I'll stick my oar in.

Yes they can be regarded as opposites in the physical sense that extropy
= order, entropy = disorder, but they are not opposed to each other in
sense that an increase in disorder much reduce the amount of order
around.  ie order + disorder is not conserved.  Thus the 2nd law of
thermodynamics (disorder increases with time) does not imply that order
must decrease, as one poster here often implies.  (Personally I rather
vaguely speculate that outbreaks of spontaneous order, complexity etc
may require associated increases of in total entropy, in which case
extropy *needs* entropy.  Local patches of order may only exist by
increasing total entropy.)

In PR terms I think it would be a mistake to present extropy and entropy
as opposites, because this will always make some people think that we're
trying to defy the 2nd Law of thermodynamics - which is impossible, as
many other people realise and who may get turned off the idea as a
result before investigating further.

Better to stick with extropy being used as a metaphor for things like
spontaneous orders, complexity, etc etc rather than as a precise
physical "thing".

So my answer is "No", "extropy" is not the opposite of "entropy".
Don't even *mention* entropy, instead talk about complexity theory,
emergent or spontaneous orders etc.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

